\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [17592186392973 :question/worked-solution "The $3$rd row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+2\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186392975 :answer/value "2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186392976 :answer/value "-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186392977 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186392978 :answer/value "-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186392979 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393093 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 3 & \\,1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & \\,2 \\\\ 11 & 3 & \\,-3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393094 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 0 & \\,1 \\\\ 10 & -1 & \\,0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & \\,2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393095 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 0 & \\,1 \\\\ 10 & -1 & \\,0 \\\\ 11 & 0 & \\,-3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393096 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & \\,1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & \\,0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & \\,2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393097 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 1 & \\,0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & \\,2 \\\\ 11 & 3 & \\,-3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393100 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393101 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393102 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393103 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393104 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393119 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $z=1$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x=y$ (or $x-y=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =i+j \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393126 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $y=-2$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x=z-1$ (or $x-z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393348 :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-4)x+6y+(-1)z &=2 \\\\ 7x+0y+(-8)z &=12 \\\\ 3x+5y+1z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{z}$, we replace the third column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n{D}_{z}=\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186393350 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393351 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393352 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393353 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393354 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 12 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393681 :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y &=5 \\\\ x-3y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$ we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393683 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186393684 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186393685 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186393686 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186393687 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186393709 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [17592186393709 :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{14}+5\\cdot {C}_{24}+0\\cdot {C}_{34}+0\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1)^{2+4}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393711 :answer/value "5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393712 :answer/value "8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393713 :answer/value "3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393714 :answer/value "5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393715 :answer/value "2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393716 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [17592186393716 :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{23}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+\\sqrt{5}\\cdot {C}_{43} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-1)^{4+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393718 :answer/value "-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393719 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393720 :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393721 :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393722 :answer/value "-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186393947 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(t){y}^{′}+Q(t)y=f(t).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${t}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4}{t}{y}^{′}+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}y=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{t}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=\\frac{1}{t},\\,{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{′}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =-\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{2}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}, \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{1}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=-\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(1-t){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot (1-t){e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}{e}^{t}+(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}){e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394627 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{pmatrix}20 \\\\ 16\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186394835 :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394836 :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394837 :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394838 :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -7\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394839 :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394840 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186394840 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries below ${a}_{12}=-1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 7 & 7\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 7 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}11 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 7 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}11 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 7 & 7\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394842 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}11 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 7 & 7\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394843 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394844 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}11 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 11 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 10\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394845 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394846 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394854 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{42}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186394854 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{42}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-2){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394856 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394857 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394858 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394859 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394860 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -5\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394862 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -7\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394863 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394864 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394865 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394866 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186394936 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}({x}^{2})^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}^{′}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2x\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right){x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}(4x({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+({x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+24)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x), \\\\ v(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)(-\\frac{1}{4}(4x({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+({x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+24)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))+({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(-x+\\frac{6}{x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+3-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(\\frac{6}{x}-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(3-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394943 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}^{′}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\right)\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6){e}^{x}, \\\\ v(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)(-\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6){e}^{x})+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1){e}^{x}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2x}){e}^{x} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395658 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1),0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1) & 1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1) & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)-0,-({sin}^{2}⁡(2t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2t-1)),0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),-1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395672 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(1) & =T(1)\\times N(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\times \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨3,-1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}⟨(2\\sqrt{2}-(-\\sqrt{2})),-(-2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}),(1-3)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}⟨2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2},-(-5\\sqrt{2}),-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}⟨3\\sqrt{2},5\\sqrt{2},-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{12}⟨3,5,-\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨3,5,-\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395679 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\times N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1,\\sqrt{3}⟩\\times \\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨(1+3),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨4,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395693 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0,-(-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}),-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186396237 :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & k\\end{vmatrix} & =k\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & k\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(3k-6)+0+0 \\\\  & =k(3k-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0.$ So,  we have:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(3k-6) & =0 \\\\ 3k(k-2) & =0 \\\\ k & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=0$ and $k=2.$"] [17592186396532 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186396533 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186396534 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186396535 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186396558 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186396559 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ \\pi  & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186396560 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186396561 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"]}
